Q: How to test a randomized input used to determine a final result on assertions? A: <p><em><strong>Preface:</strong> Your question indicates you're testing student code. This answer assumes you have a reference implementation that returns correct results for a given input that you can test against the students' results.</em></p>
<hr />
<p>You mention:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I know that I could write separate fixed tests or use a <code>@ParameterizedTest</code> for known cases, but the loop is meant to give broad deterministic coverage without manually listing every input.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>From that, I gather you think a parameterized test's arguments need to be manually defined ahead of time. This is not true. The arguments can be generated lazily. And given you know the checksum for <em>all</em> the expected outputs, I can only assume you have a reference implementation that gives the expected output for each given input. Meaning the expected output can be generated lazily as well.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Random;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;
import java.util.stream.Stream;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.Arguments;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.MethodSource;

// By default, @MethodSource looks for a static method in the same
// class, and with the same name, as the parameterized test method.
static Stream&lt;Arguments&gt; testMethod() {
  var rng = new Random(12345);
  return IntStream.range(0, 10_000).mapToObj(_ -&gt; {
    var input = generateInput(rng);
    return Arguments.of(input);
  });
}

@ParameterizedTest
@MethodSource
void testMethod(int[] input) {
  int expected = teacherMethod(input);
  int actual = studentMethod(input);
  assertEquals(
      expected,
      actual,
      () -&gt; &quot;'studentMethod' returned wrong result for input: &quot; + Arrays.toString(input));
}
</code></pre>
<p><sup>– If you want to generate the arguments ahead of time there are other argument sources, such as <a href="https://docs.junit.org/6.1.1/api/org.junit.jupiter.params/org/junit/jupiter/params/provider/CsvFileSource.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>@CsvFileSource</code></a>, that can enable that. You may need to use <a href="https://docs.junit.org/6.1.1/api/org.junit.jupiter.params/org/junit/jupiter/params/converter/ConvertWith.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>@ConvertWith</code></a> to parse the &quot;input column&quot; into an <code>int[]</code>. Also, note that you could make the <code>expected</code> value an argument of the parameterized test if that makes sense for you to do.</sup></p>
<p>JUnit considers each invocation of a <code>@ParameterizedTest</code> method as a separate test. By default, the name of each test will include the test method's arguments. Those arguments will even be associated with the parameter name if they were kept during compilation. Regardless, even if you change the name to not include the arguments, the above example puts the input in the (lazy) assertion message so one can always see it in the report/logs. The expected and actual values will also be included in the message.</p>
<p>Though that also means the JUnit report will have 10,000 tests. Whether those are easy to browse depends on how the report was generated. Gradle generates a nice HTML page that can filter out successful tests so only the failed ones are visible, and each test with output gets its own page that isolates said output. I'm not sure how the generated report looks in Maven. An IDE typically has its own view for tests.</p>
<p>You can also generate your own report. For instance, by implementing your own <a href="https://docs.junit.org/6.1.1/api/org.junit.platform.launcher/org/junit/platform/launcher/TestExecutionListener.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>TestExecutionListener</code></a>, by implementing an extension such as <a href="https://docs.junit.org/6.1.1/api/org.junit.jupiter.api/org/junit/jupiter/api/extension/TestWatcher.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>TestWatcher</code></a>, by enabling <a href="https://docs.junit.org/6.1.1/advanced-topics/junit-platform-reporting.html#open-test-reporting" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Open Test Reporting</a> and parsing the XML, or by some other mechanism I'm not aware of.</p>
<hr />
<p>That all said, you also ask:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What is a good pattern in JUnit that will allow me to keep my random testing deterministic, report on the very first input which caused the mismatch, or ideally reduce the input down to the smallest possible counter example?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The above example does not appear to match these requirements exactly. It reports each input that caused a local mismatch and continues to test each input until all inputs are tested. It also abandons your checksum approach in favor of testing each input individually. But given my understanding of your scenario, I think the above example is the better approach.</p>
